From: GoldCorpHF firmware project
Subject: [PATCH] usb: musb: host: recycle a bound qh left without URBs in
 endpoint_disable

A Xiegu X6200 (Allwinner R16, musb in host mode on its HOST port, Linux
6.1.82) hung for good when a USB hub with a HackRF and an RTL-SDR, both
streaming, browned out: about 117 "musb-hdrc ... epN RX three-strikes
error" lines and repeated hub disconnects in 10 s, then

  usb 3-1.1: USB disconnect, device number 39
  Unable to handle kernel NULL pointer dereference at virtual address 00000004
  Internal error: Oops: 5 [#1] PREEMPT SMP ARM
  Workqueue: usb_hub_wq hub_event
  PC is at musb_h_disable+0x108/0x17c [musb_hdrc]
  LR is at musb_h_disable+0x18/0x17c [musb_hdrc]
  r0 : 00000000 ... Flags: nZCv  IRQs off

The oops is taken with musb->lock held and interrupts off on that CPU:
no ping, screen frozen, power cycle needed.

In the radio's musb_hdrc.ko, musb_h_disable+0x108 is "ldr r3, [r0, #4]",
the load of urb->unlinked right after urb = next_urb(qh) in the branch
where the hardware endpoint is still bound to this qh; r0 stays 0 when
the endpoint's URB list is empty. usbcore always empties that list
(usb_hcd_flush_endpoint) before it calls endpoint_disable, so any qh that
musb_h_disable() finds still bound has no URB, and next_urb() is NULL.

Such a qh is one that was not recycled as its last URB went. One way:
musb_advance_schedule() and musb_urb_dequeue() each save qh->is_ready,
clear it and drop the lock for the giveback, then restore it. When the
interrupt handler completes one URB on one CPU while the class driver
unlinks another on a second CPU, the side that saved the cleared value
and restores last leaves is_ready at 0 with URBs still queued. Nothing
starts them; musb_urb_dequeue() then gives each back down its
"!qh->is_ready" path, which recycles the qh only when the saved
is_ready was set, so the qh stays bound to the hardware endpoint with an
empty list until the device goes.

A NULL check alone (as posted to linux-usb in 2016, "usb: musb: host:
Fix NULL pointer dereference in SMP environment", not merged) frees the
qh but leaves hw_ep->in_qh/out_qh pointing at it. Recycle it the way
musb_advance_schedule() does when a qh's last URB completes instead:
abort and release its DMA channel, flush the endpoint, mark it for
reprogramming, unbind and free the qh, and start the next qh waiting for
that endpoint (control, multiplexed bulk), so a device that stays behind
on a shared bulk endpoint keeps streaming.

The is_ready race itself is left as it is: this only keeps endpoint
disable from oopsing on what it leaves behind.
---
--- a/drivers/usb/musb/musb_host.c
+++ b/drivers/usb/musb/musb_host.c
@@ -2416,6 +2416,65 @@
 	return ret;
 }
 
+/*
+ * Recycle a qh that still holds its hardware endpoint with no URB queued,
+ * as musb_advance_schedule() does when a qh's last URB completes: stop the
+ * endpoint, unbind and free the qh, and start the next qh waiting for that
+ * endpoint (control, multiplexed bulk).
+ *
+ * usbcore empties an endpoint's URB list before disabling it, so a qh
+ * musb_h_disable() finds bound is always one that was not recycled as its
+ * last URB went. That happens when two CPUs give URBs of one qh back at
+ * once: musb_advance_schedule() and musb_urb_dequeue() each save is_ready,
+ * clear it and drop the lock for the giveback, and the one restoring last
+ * can leave it 0 with the qh still bound; the URBs left are then given back
+ * by musb_urb_dequeue() without recycling it. Seen on an Allwinner R16 with
+ * two SDRs streaming through a hub in a brown-out (three-strikes errors,
+ * disconnects): next_urb() returned NULL here and the oops was taken with
+ * the controller lock held and interrupts off, hanging the system.
+ *
+ * Context: controller locked, irqs blocked
+ */
+static void musb_h_disable_idle_qh(struct musb *musb, struct musb_qh *qh,
+				   int is_in)
+{
+	struct musb_hw_ep	*ep = qh->hw_ep;
+	struct dma_controller	*dma = musb->dma_controller;
+	struct dma_channel	**channel;
+	struct list_head	*head = NULL;
+	struct musb_qh		*next;
+
+	musb_ep_select(musb->mregs, ep->epnum);
+	channel = is_in ? &ep->rx_channel : &ep->tx_channel;
+	if (*channel) {
+		dma->channel_abort(*channel);
+		dma->channel_release(*channel);
+		*channel = NULL;
+	}
+	if (!ep->epnum)
+		musb_h_ep0_flush_fifo(ep);
+	else if (is_in)
+		musb_h_flush_rxfifo(ep, 0);
+	else
+		musb_h_tx_flush_fifo(ep);
+	if (is_in)
+		ep->rx_reinit = 1;
+	else
+		ep->tx_reinit = 1;
+
+	musb_ep_set_qh(ep, is_in, NULL);
+	qh->hep->hcpriv = NULL;
+	if (qh->mux == 1)
+		head = qh->type == USB_ENDPOINT_XFER_CONTROL ? &musb->control
+			: is_in ? &musb->in_bulk : &musb->out_bulk;
+	list_del(&qh->ring);
+	kfree(qh);
+
+	next = head ? first_qh(head) : NULL;
+	if (next && next->is_ready && next_urb(next))
+		musb_start_urb(musb, is_in, next);
+}
+
 /* disable an endpoint */
 static void
 musb_h_disable(struct usb_hcd *hcd, struct usb_host_endpoint *hep)
@@ -2438,6 +2497,10 @@
 	qh->is_ready = 0;
 	if (musb_ep_get_qh(qh->hw_ep, is_in) == qh) {
 		urb = next_urb(qh);
+		if (!urb) {
+			musb_h_disable_idle_qh(musb, qh, is_in);
+			goto exit;
+		}
 
 		/* make software (then hardware) stop ASAP */
 		if (!urb->unlinked)
